}

The location of the environment can be found at
\code{tools::R_user_dir("reticulate", "cache")}.

See the documentation for \code{\link[reticulate:py_require]{reticulate::py_require()}} to learn more about this
method.
}

\subsection{Persistent Environment with \code{install_tabpfn()}}{

Alternatively, \code{\link[=install_tabpfn]{install_tabpfn()}} creates a persistent virtual environment
named \code{"r-tabpfn"} and installs the Python \code{tabpfn} library into it:

\preformatted{
  library(tabpfn)

  # Install the latest release
  install_tabpfn()

  # Or pin a specific version for reproducibility
  install_tabpfn(version = "2.0.9")
}

You do not need to call \code{use_virtualenv()} afterwards: because this package
imports the Python module \code{"tabpfn"}, \pkg{reticulate} automatically
discovers and prefers the \code{"r-tabpfn"} environment over the ephemeral one.
Run \code{install_tabpfn()} before \pkg{tabpfn} has initialized Python (i.e.,
before fitting a model); if Python is already loaded, restart R first.
}

}

\subsection{Data}{

Each model version was pre-trained on data up to a certain size, and those
sizes have grown a great deal across versions. The \emph{Data limits by version}
section below has the numbers.

These limits are enforced by the Python library, which raises when data
exceeds them. \pkg{tabpfn} does not check them itself, so the error you see
names the model actually loaded.

Predictors do not require preprocessing; missing values and factor vectors
are allowed.
}

\subsection{Model Selection}{

By default, TabPFN uses the Python library's current default model version.
There are two ways to override this.
\subsection{Selecting a model version}{

Use the \code{version} argument to select a specific released model version:

\preformatted{
  mod <- tab_pfn(predictors, outcome, version = "v2.5")

  # A bare number works too
  mod <- tab_pfn(predictors, outcome, version = 3.5)
}

New model versions are released from time to time, so rather than listing
them here, call \code{\link[=tabpfn_list_versions]{tabpfn_list_versions()}} to see what your installed Python
library offers:

\preformatted{
  > tabpfn_list_versions()
  [1] "v2"    "v2.5"  "v2.6"  "v3"    "v3.5"  "v3.5-fast"
}
}

\subsection{Pointing to a local model file}{

If you have a model file on disk (e.g., downloaded for offline use), pass
its path via \code{control_tab_pfn(model_path = ...)}:

\preformatted{
  ctrl <- control_tab_pfn(model_path = "/path/to/model_file.ckpt")
  mod  <- tab_pfn(predictors, outcome, control = ctrl)
}

Note that \code{version} and \code{model_path} are mutually exclusive: if \code{version}
is set, it overwrites any \code{model_path} supplied through \code{control}.
}

}

\subsection{Calculations}{

For the \code{softmax_temperature} value, the softmax terms are:

\preformatted{
exp(value / softmax_temperature)
}

A value of \code{softmax_temperature = 1} results in a plain softmax value.
}
}
\section{Data limits by version}{
\tabular{lllll}{
   Version \tab Rows (GPU) \tab Rows (CPU) \tab Predictors \tab Classes \cr
   \code{"v3.5-fast"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 20K \tab 160 \cr
   \code{"v3.5"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 20K \tab 160 \cr
   \code{"v3"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 2K \tab 160 \cr
   \code{"v2.6"} \tab 100K \tab 1K \tab 2K \tab 10 \cr
   \code{"v2.5"} \tab 50K \tab 1K \tab 2K \tab 10 \cr
   \code{"v2"} \tab 10K \tab 1K \tab 500 \tab 10 \cr
}


The CPU column is not advice. TabPFN refuses a CPU fit above that many
rows, whatever the version's own limit says, so \code{"v3.5"} stops at 5,000
rows on a machine without a GPU. Set \code{ignore_pretraining_limits = TRUE}
in \code{\link[=control_tab_pfn]{control_tab_pfn()}}, or the \code{TABPFN_ALLOW_CPU_LARGE_DATASET}
environment variable, to lift it. The fit then runs, slowly.

Every limit here is enforced by the Python library, which raises an error
naming the count and the limit. Use \code{training_set_limit} to fit on a
sample instead.

The row and predictor maxima trade off against each other, so you cannot
always reach both at once. The ceiling is not a promise either: for
\code{"v3.5"}, PriorLabs recommends up to 6,000 predictors even though the
model tops out at 20,000. See \url{https://docs.priorlabs.ai/models}.
}

